<p><strong>Summoned by PM:</strong></p>
<p>Hi there Noah J Cooper. Let’s take a look at your concept.</p>
<p>So my initial comment to you (through PM) was that the concept hasn’t been fleshed out fully, and I still stand by that assessment. You should endeavour to use all 400 words of the limit, and it’s a red flag if you haven’t done that: it usually means the concept isn’t able to stand on its two feet.</p>
<p>True to nature, I’m seeing a few issues with the concept, which I will list down below:</p>
<p>(1) When I read your elevator pitch, I don’t really understand the mechanics of how the scp operates. Okay, so it explodes and takes over people. What does it mean to “get access to all of the previous hosts memories” and how does the sphere “regenerate”? More importantly, why does this happen? What is the sphere’s purpose in doing this? All these questions could have been explored further.</p>
<p>(2) Under your “Central Narrative” segment, instead of telling us what <em>could</em> be shown in the logs, tell us explicitly <em>what</em> the story is. How exactly does the Foundation discover the scp and contain it? Do they experiment with the scp? What happens when they do? How does your article end? Give us specifics please.</p>
<p>(3) I don’t understand either what you mean at the end when you say “so in a way, it relies on the foundation to survive.” Perhaps it’s because I don’t understand how the scp functions in the first place, so I couldn’t see how the sphere relies on the Foundation to survive. Doesn’t it regenerate on its own as well? So again, I’m left scratching my head.</p>
<p>If you decide to revise the concept further / respond below, do drop me another PM and I’ll try my best to reply. Best of luck!</p>

