														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4822974">
									

<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Genre (Optional):</strong> N/A</p>
<p><strong>Page Layout (Optional):</strong> Standard SCP Article. Name, Classification, Containment procedures, description.</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> This SCP is a beach sixty miles south of Long Beach, California upon which, no matter how long an individual walks across the beach, the water can never be reached. However, the water always appears to be no more than eighty to ninety yards away. The actual anomaly is slightly more interesting than that, as you'll see in the central narrative below.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> Through a series of audio logs between expeditionary personnel and command, as well as autopsy reports, readers will follow teams of research personnel sent to explore SCP-XXXX in hopes to discover more about its anomalous nature. The first expeditionary log reveals that the team suffers a casualty by &quot;dry-drowning&quot; around eight km out, and the others are recovered. Autopsy reveals cause of death to be asphyxia by drowning. The second log reveals that the use of a SCBA allows explorers to venture out substantially farther, reaching close to twenty six kilometers before their oxygen tanks reach half-way empty and they have to turn around. The third log follows a team of researchers more well equipped, with oxygen tanks that should last forty-eight hours. The team reports back around fifty-three km out that there is some form of writing in the sand. Later, they report that they can see what they believe to be a boat in the distance, just off shore, however at the fifty-seven km mark three of the five oxygen tanks simultaneously malfunction. The team turns around, holding their breath unless breathing in through the SCBA, which they take turns using to survive. All team members are recovered, and the writing in the sand is sent to linguists for deciphering. Log four, the final log before SCP-XXXX is deemed unfit for exploration, documents the exploration of four research personnel on board a semi-aquatic vehicle designed to withstand high pressure and impact. They make it twenty-six km before the vehicle begins to flood with water. The team is forced to abandon the vehicle and carry on. At the fifty-seven km mark, the lead researcher calls in that there appears to be a tall, disproportionate figure down near the water, henceforth denoted as SCP-XXXX-1. Before command gets a chance to respond, all personnel experience simultaneous SCBA failures, and the hissing of air and screaming of researchers can be heard just before comm connection is lost. Three of the four corpses are recovered and autopsied, and cause of death is determined to be asphyxia by drowning. The fourth cable comes back severed, and there is blood and a torn shred of clothing on the cable.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> A beach that kills people by &quot;dry drowning&quot; them is certainly unique. The test logs will serve as something to actually keep readers interested.</p>
<p><strong>Additional Notes:</strong> Researchers in the audio logs are tethered by spools of cable so they can be recovered. All feedback still welcome.</p>
<p><em>I have attached a draft of an audio log (albeit not a great one, it is still a draft) for posterity</em><br />
[<a href="http://scp-sandbox-3.wikidot.com/buckshot74">http://scp-sandbox-3.wikidot.com/buckshot74</a>]</p>

